            <h2>Introduction & How to Use This Roadmap</h2>
            
            <div class="alert">
                <div class="alert-title">🎯 Getting Started</div>
                <p>This document is a semester-by-semester blueprint for an ECE undergraduate who wants to end college with real options — a Tier-1 core hardware offer (VLSI / embedded / semiconductor), a Tier-1 software product offer (Google / Amazon-tier SDE / quant), or both.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Four Principles That Make This Work</h3>
            <div class="principles">
                <div class="principle">
                    <strong>1. Consistency > Intensity</strong>
                    <p>A student who codes 1 hour a day for 4 years beats a student who binges 12 hours before every interview. Every weekly plan here assumes ~5–7 focused hours outside class.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="principle">
                    <strong>2. Projects > Textbooks</strong>
                    <p>Recruiters cannot verify your CGPA in 30 minutes, but they can look at your GitHub. Ship one small project per month, one flagship per year.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="principle">
                    <strong>3. Depth in One Track, Literacy in All</strong>
                    <p>By Sem 5 you must pick a primary track (VLSI / Embedded / SDE / Quant). But keep DSA and one core electronics subject fresh throughout — it is your safety net.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="principle">
                    <strong>4. Language & Communication</strong>
                    <p>English fluency is the base level. German (or French/Japanese) unlocks Bosch, Siemens, Infineon, BMW, Mercedes R&D. Practise talking about your projects out loud.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

